# A capacitor charging and discharging, over and over: a slow square wave through a resistor into it.
#
# For five seconds the source is at 9 V and the capacitor charges: quickly at first, then more and more
# slowly, as the voltage left across the resistor, and so the current, shrinks. After one time constant,
# R × C = 10 kΩ × 100 µF = 1 s, it has reached 63% of the way; after five it is all but full. Then the
# source drops to 0 V and the capacitor gives its charge back through the same resistor, falling the
# same curve upside down: 63% of the way down in a second, all but empty in five. Then it starts again.
#
# M2, across the resistor, shows the current: a jump to its largest the moment the source changes, then
# dying away as the capacitor catches up, one way while it charges and the other while it discharges.
# Turn R1 or C1 up and the curves stretch, so the capacitor no longer fills or empties before the source
# turns round; turn them down and they sharpen towards the square itself.

speed 1

source    V1  0 0  0 6  square 0 9 0.1
resistor  R1  0 0  6 0  10k
capacitor C1  6 0  6 6  100u
wire          6 6  0 6
ground        0 6
label     cap 6 0
meter     M1  6 0  6 6
meter     M2  0 0  6 0
